# Copy-on-write

After `fork()`, parent and child share every physical page. The kernel marks the PTEs read-only in both. When either writes, the CPU faults; the handler sees the page is COW, allocates a new frame, copies the 4 KiB, maps it writable in the writer and drops the share count.

Why it matters: most forks are followed immediately by `exec`, which throws the address space away. Copying everything eagerly would be wasted work. `vfork` was the older hack for the same problem.

Other places COW shows up:
- `mmap(MAP_PRIVATE)` of a file;
- the zero page: all untouched anonymous memory maps one shared zero-filled frame until written;
- file systems like Btrfs and ZFS (never overwrite a live block);
- snapshots of VMs and containers;
- persistent data structures in functional languages.

Gotcha: a process that forks with a huge heap and then writes everywhere (Redis BGSAVE under heavy writes) can double its memory use.
